        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-e355c9c0c8a97c91\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Birkin’s repeated “I love you” clearly avows love (T) while also enacting his qualified acquiescence to Ursula’s demand that love be sufficient for their relationship (P). The deliberate, subtly ironic response to her prompting is not presented as a reflex-like expressive discharge (E).\",\"ambiguity\":\"The performative force is qualified: Birkin undertakes to accept Ursula’s conception of their bond, but his irony, submission, and “then” preserve reluctance rather than an unreserved pledge.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Birkin directly addresses Ursula, repeating “I love you” while embracing her, in response to her request that he say it.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Ursula explicitly solicits the declaration after a dispute over whether love is enough. Birkin has already affirmed that he loves her but wants their relation to be “something else”; here he yields to her position, with the narration marking a mixture of love, irony, and submission. Thus the declaration is sincere as an avowal yet relationally concessive and ambivalent, rather than an uncontrolled affective outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier Birkin says, “I love you right enough,” while adding, “But I want it to be something else.”\",\"supports\":\"Strong truth-conditional support and evidence that his objection concerns the form of the relationship, not the existence of his love.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Ursula repeatedly asks, “Say ‘my love’ to me, say it, say it,” and insists, “We can only love each other.”\",\"supports\":\"The classified utterance is a responsive, deliberately elicited declaration, which supports relational uptake but weighs against reflex-like expressive force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He murmurs “in a subtle voice of love, and irony, and submission”: “Let love be enough then. I love you then—I love you. I’m bored by the rest.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration both avows love and enacts qualified submission to making love sufficient; the controlled irony and explicit concession do not supply independent E force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love construction in Chapter XIII of D. H. Lawrence’s Women in Love.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the target occurrence and its canonical textual setting.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the avowal and P captures the qualified relational undertaking implicit in “Let love be enough then.” Delivery, irony, and ambivalence are adequately represented as context, with no residual core force requiring E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
